window.docContents[78].resolve({"/Elliptic-Equations/Energy-and-Weak-Boundary-Value-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Energy-and-Weak-Boundary-Value-Problems--Weak-Elliptic-Problems--Weak-Dirichlet-Problems":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Elliptic Equations\u0009Energy and Weak Boundary-Value Problems\u0009Weak Elliptic Problems","header":"5.4.2.3. Weak Dirichlet Problems","id":"/Elliptic-Equations/Energy-and-Weak-Boundary-Value-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Energy-and-Weak-Boundary-Value-Problems--Weak-Elliptic-Problems--Weak-Dirichlet-Problems"},"/Foundations/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Foundational-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Coverage-conventions":{"contents":"The entries distinguish three kinds of coverage. A topic\nis formalized when PDEAtlas states and proves it, inherited when the\nBlueprint cites the Mathlib declarations directly, without local restatements,\nand partial when the reusable core is formalized in a stated range or\ngeometric setting; each partial entry names that range.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Foundational Coverage and Open Problems","header":"3.5.1.1. Coverage conventions","id":"/Foundations/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Foundational-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Coverage-conventions"},"/Parabolic-Equations/Classical-Heat-Flow/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Classical-Heat-Flow--The-Heat-Operator-and-Classical-Problems":{"contents":"The classical heat equation couples a first time derivative to a second\nspatial derivative. This asymmetry determines the geometry of the problem:\ntime has a preferred direction, initial data live on the time-zero face, and\nthe spatial Laplacian acts on each fixed-time slice. Coordinate zero is time\nthroughout.\n\nThe declarations here state equations and initial traces. Explicit\nwhole-space solutions come from the Gaussian kernel and Duhamel's formula, and\nthe variational theory replaces pointwise derivatives by Bochner-space\nidentities on bounded domains.\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Classical Heat Flow","header":"6.2.1. The Heat Operator and Classical Problems","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/Classical-Heat-Flow/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Classical-Heat-Flow--The-Heat-Operator-and-Classical-Problems"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Sobolev-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Fractional-spaces-on-domains":{"contents":"Three domain constructions describe different ways to control a function\nnear the boundary.\n\n1. HsRestrictionSpace is the complete real Hilbert quotient of whole-space\n by the kernel of restriction into .  It is available\nat nonnegative order, carries the infimum-over-extensions quotient norm,\nand has a canonical least-norm whole-space extension.\nhsRestrictionSubmodule retains the corresponding raw-function membership\ninterface.2. GagliardoSpace is the complete real Hilbert graph space of the intrinsic\ndifference quotient on .\ngagliardoSubmodule retains the corresponding raw-function membership\ninterface. Its intended Sobolev interpretation is .3. HsSupportedClosureSpace is the closed complex subspace of whole-space\n generated by Schwartz functions supported in .  It is\ncomplete for every real order, but its elements remain whole-space\ndistributions.\n\nFor  and , the completed whole-space Gagliardo model is\ncontinuously linearly equivalent to . The Fourier\nseminorm identity supplies the norm comparison, and the equivalence preserves\nthe underlying  function in both directions.\n\nFor any domain , the same comparison identifies the quotient of\nwhole-space Gagliardo functions by the restriction kernel with\nHsRestrictionSpace. Restricting a whole-space representative then gives a\nbounded injective map into the intrinsic GagliardoSpace on ,\npreserving the physical  class. Its range is exactly the intrinsic\nfunctions that admit a whole-space Gagliardo extension.\n\nFor a half-space, even reflection supplies the extension. It doubles the\nsquared  norm and increases the squared fractional seminorm by at most\na factor of four, so its operator norm is at most . Combining reflection\nwith the whole-space Fourier comparison gives a continuous linear\nequivalence between the intrinsic and restriction spaces, for every\n and . Both directions preserve the underlying\n class.\n\nOn a domain with a  extension atlas, in positive dimension and for\n, the reflected chart extensions and the interior zero extension glue\ninto a bounded intrinsic extension operator. The same argument then makes\nthe canonical map from the restriction space to the intrinsic Gagliardo space\na continuous linear equivalence. Both\ndirections preserve the physical  class, and the inverse norm\nis bounded by the whole-space comparison factor times the norm of the atlas\nextension.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Sobolev Coverage and Open Problems","header":"4.6.1.13. Fractional spaces on domains","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Sobolev-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Fractional-spaces-on-domains"}});